WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.200
One of the worst pieces of advice that I

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:04.920
see in entrepreneurship is like do

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.840
something you're passionate about, do

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.040
something you find interesting.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.080
Um

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:11.080
I think that's a terrible way to go

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:12.520
about it because if you're operating any

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:13.960
business at scale, you're dealing with

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:15.520
problems, you're hiring, you're firing,

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:16.720
you're delegating. So, you might as well

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.400
pick one where you're actually likely to

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:20.240
make some money because business without

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:21.960
any money is

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:23.400
damn near impossible. But when you start

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:25.400
cash flowing and you start rolling that

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.440
snowball down the hill and you're making

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:29.000
$10,000 a month, $50,000 a month,

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:31.040
$100,000 a month, a million dollars a

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.599
month, business gets infinitely easier

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.160
because you can put smart

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:35.200
put smart people between you and your

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:35.760
problems.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.800
>> Mhm.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:40.000
>> So, pick a business where you can win.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:41.240
Um and choose your competition. Like

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:42.480
look out there. We we got in the

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:44.360
self-storage business because we said,

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.850
"Okay, where are all the really dumb

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.480
people making money?"

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:48.720
>> [laughter]

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:50.560
>> Uh where can we be average?

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:52.840
>> That's a great question to ask, yeah.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:53.240
Where can we be average and still win?

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:53.960
>> Yeah.

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.160
>> Yeah.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:56.440
>> Yeah, so do you want to go compete

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:57.960
against the folks in Silicon Valley

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:59.360
with, you know,

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:01.520
$20 million of venture capital funding

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:03.080
and computer science degrees and parents

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:04.120
in country clubs or do you want to

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:06.040
compete against the person down the

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:07.320
street who's making $2 or $3 million a

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:07.840
year but still has a fax machine on

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.080
their desk?

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:11.280
>> And doesn't even have a website.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.760
Yeah, right. Right.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:14.920
>> All business is hard. All business is

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:17.160
hard, but some are easier than others.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:19.040
>> Yeah, and by the way, those $20 million

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.920
venture-backed startups with with that

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:23.640
has a founding team of four who all have

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:26.680
MBAs from Stanford, those companies

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:29.760
still fail at like a 95%

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:32.280
failure rate. So, even though it seems

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:33.960
glamorous to be in that vehicle,

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.760
it's actually not even for the people

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:37.800
that are doing it at the highest level.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:39.480
So, there's other ways

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:42.080
>> The most popular page on my website of

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:44.480
all time is my list of 200 plus business

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:46.120
ideas that are basically all

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:48.520
bootstrappable, like have been done

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:50.680
before, are boring. Um so, you can go to

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:52.760
Indie Startup.com/ideas and check that

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:53.840
out, but yeah, man, look, it's power

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:55.120
washing, it's cleaning something that's

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:56.360
dirty, it's picking up something and

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:58.360
moving it to where it needs to go, It's

00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:00.600
repairing something that's broken.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:02.160
And any number of niches and industries

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:05.720
is a great place to start if you have no

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:07.080
money, no network, and no operational

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:08.600
chops. Like to to run a business at

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:11.200
scale, you need three things. You need

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:12.640
cash because doing it it is expensive,

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:14.320
hiring is expensive, investing in

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:16.000
everything. You need a network of people

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:16.840
you can hire, people you can sell to,

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.840
and then you need to actually know what

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:19.920
you're doing. You need to be able to

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:21.120
make decisions and run a company. A lot

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:21.880
of people have none of those three

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:22.160
things.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:23.600
